      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve for \\( a \\) when \\( g(a) = 0 \\) for the function \\( g(x) = 3x - 4 \\), we need to set the function equal to 0 and solve for \\( a \\). Let's follow these steps:\n\n1. Start with the given equation:\n   \\[\n   g(a) = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( g(x) \\) with \\( 3x - 4 \\):\n   \\[\n   3a - 4 = 0\n   \\]\n\n3. Solve for \\( a \\):\n   \\[\n   3a - 4 = 0\n   \\]\n   Add 4 to both sides of the equation:\n   \\[\n   3a = 4\n   \\]\n   Divide both sides by 3:\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( a \\) that satisfies \\( g(a) = 0 \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{4}{3}}\\).\n\nFind the smallest positive period of the function \\( f(x) = \\sin 2x + 2 \\cos x + 1 \\).\n\nTo determine the smallest positive period of the function \\( f(x) = \\sin 2x + 2 \\cos x + 1 \\), we need to identify the periods of the individual components of the function and then find the least common multiple (LCM) of these periods.\n\n1. **Identify the period of \\(\\sin 2x\\):**\n   The function \\(\\sin 2x\\) is a sine function with an argument \\(2x\\). The period of \\(\\sin kx\\) is \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{k}\\). Here, \\(k = 2\\), so the period of \\(\\sin 2x\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the period of \\(2 \\cos x\\):**\n   The function \\(2 \\cos x\\) is a cosine function. The period of \\(\\cos x\\) is \\(2\\pi\\), so the period of \\(2 \\cos x\\) is also:\n   \\[\n   2\\pi\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the period of the sum \\(f(x) = \\sin 2x + 2 \\cos x + 1\\):**\n   The function \\(f(x)\\) is a sum of \\(\\sin 2x\\) and \\(2 \\cos x\\), both of which have periods of \\(\\pi\\) and \\(2\\pi\\) respectively. The period of the sum of these functions is the least common multiple (LCM) of their individual periods. The LCM of \\(\\pi\\) and \\(2\\pi\\) is \\(2\\pi\\).\n\nTherefore, the smallest positive period of the function \\( f(x) = \\sin 2x + 2 \\cos x + 1 \\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\).\n\nIf \\( M = \\left\\{ x \\mid 2^{x+1} \\leq 8^{x} \\right\\} \\) and \\( N = \\left\\{ y \\mid y = \\log_{2} x, x \\in M \\right\\} \\), find the number of elements in the set \\( M \\cap N \\).\n\nTo solve the problem, we first need to determine the set \\( M \\) by solving the inequality \\( 2^{x+1} \\leq 8^x \\).\n\nRecall that \\( 8^x = (2^3)^x = 2^{3x} \\). Therefore, the inequality becomes:\n\\[ 2^{x+1} \\leq 2^{3x}. \\]\nSince the bases are the same, we can compare the exponents directly:\n\\[ x + 1 \\leq 3x. \\]\nSolving for \\( x \\), we get:\n\\[ 1 \\leq 2x \\implies \\frac{1}{2} \\leq x. \\]\nThus, \\( x \\geq \\frac{1}{2} \\). Therefore, the set \\( M \\) is:\n\\[ M = \\left\\{ x \\mid x \\geq \\frac{1}{2} \\right\\}. \\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the set \\( N \\). The set \\( N \\) is defined as:\n\\[ N = \\left\\{ y \\mid y = \\log_2 x, x \\in M \\right\\}. \\]\nSince \\( x \\in M \\), we have \\( x \\geq \\frac{1}{2} \\). The logarithmic function \\( \\log_2 x \\) is increasing, so:\n\\[ \\log_2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\leq \\log_2 x \\leq \\log_2 \\left( \\infty \\right). \\]\nWe know that \\( \\log_2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = -1 \\) and \\( \\log_2 \\left( \\infty \\right) = \\infty \\). Therefore, the set \\( N \\) is:\n\\[ N = \\left\\{ y \\mid -1 \\leq y \\leq \\infty \\right\\}. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the intersection of the sets \\( M \\) and \\( N \\):\n\\[ M \\cap N = \\left\\{ x \\mid x \\geq \\frac{1}{2} \\right\\} \\cap \\left\\{ y \\mid -1 \\leq y \\leq \\infty \\right\\}. \\]\nSince \\( N \\) is the interval \\([-1, \\infty)\\) and \\( M \\) is the interval \\([\\frac{1}{2}, \\infty)\\), the intersection is:\n\\[ M \\cap N = \\left[ \\frac{1}{2}, \\infty \\right). \\]\n\nThe number of elements in the set \\( M \\cap N \\) is the number of elements in the interval \\(\\left[ \\frac{1}{2}, \\infty \\right)\\), which is infinite. However, if we are asked for the number of distinct elements in the intersection, we can say there are infinitely many elements.\n\nThus, the number of elements in the set \\( M \\cap N \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\infty}. \\]\n\nAn industrial loom weaves 0.128 meters of cloth every second. Approximately, how many seconds will it take for the loom to weave 23 meters of cloth?\n\nTo determine how many seconds it will take for the loom to weave 23 meters of cloth, we start by noting the rate at which the loom weaves. The loom weaves 0.128 meters of cloth every second. We need to find out how many seconds are required to weave 23 meters of cloth.\n\nWe can set up the problem as a simple division:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Total cloth}}{\\text{Cloth per second}} = \\frac{23 \\text{ meters}}{0.128 \\text{ meters/second}}\n\\]\n\nNow, we perform the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{23}{0.128} = \\frac{23 \\times 1000}{0.128 \\times 1000} = \\frac{23000}{128}\n\\]\n\nTo make the division easier, we can simplify it by performing the actual division:\n\n\\[\n23000 \\div 128 \\approx 180.546875\n\\]\n\nSince the loom can't weave for a fraction of a second in practical terms, we round up to the next whole number. Therefore, it will take approximately 181 seconds for the loom to weave 23 meters of cloth.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{181}\n\\]",
